$ MacNeal & Harder (1985), Sec. 3 - curved cantilever beam. REFINED MESH.
$
$ Same problem as curved_beam.bdf -- 90-deg arc in the XY plane, inner radius
$ 4.12, outer radius 4.32, width (radial) 0.20, shell thickness 0.10,
$ E = 1.0e7, nu = 0.25, cantilevered at theta=0, in-plane unit tip load Fy at
$ theta=90 -- on a 24 x 4 mesh instead of the published 6 x 1 one.
$
$ WHY THIS DECK EXISTS. On the standard 6x1 benchmark mesh no bilinear quad
$ reaches the curved-beam-theory target: MacNeal & Harder's own published
$ QUAD4 result is 0.833 of it, and both OpenJFEM and the reference solver sit
$ there. That is a property of the mesh, not of the element, and the only way
$ to show the difference is to refine. This deck is the convergence evidence;
$ curved_beam.bdf remains the benchmark.
$
$ MESH RULE (so this deck is reproducible without a generator):
$   node id  = 25*j + i + 1,  i in 0..24 (arc), j in 0..4 (radial)
$   theta_i  = i * 90 / 24  degrees
$   r_j      = 4.32 - j * 0.20 / 4      (j = 0 is the OUTER radius)
$   x = r cos(theta), y = r sin(theta), z = 0
$   element (i,j) = [nid(i,j), nid(i+1,j), nid(i+1,j+1), nid(i,j+1)]
$
$ Reference (in-plane Fy = 1 at tip): tip displacement Uy = 0.08734.
$ Read at the MID-WIDTH tip node (j = 2), the closest discrete analogue of
$ the beam-theory reference point.
$
SOL 101
CEND
TITLE = MacNeal-Harder curved cantilever beam (in-plane load, 24x4)
SUBCASE 1
  LABEL = In-plane unit tip load Fy at theta=90
  SPC = 1
  LOAD = 1
  DISPLACEMENT = ALL
BEGIN BULK
$ ---- solver parameters ----
$ Drilling-DOF stabilisation is not part of the benchmark definition and
$ solvers default it differently (MSC/Nastran 70.5 SOL 101: 0.0; MSC.Nastran
$ 2004+ and OpenJFEM: 100.0). Pinned so the accuracy and same-deck parity
$ comparisons are parameter-exact. See validation/README.md, "PARAM,K6ROT".
PARAM,K6ROT,0.
$ ---- material and property ----
MAT1, 1, 1.0+7, , 0.25
PSHELL, 1, 1, 0.10, 1
$ ---- grids ----
